    "title": "LESSON 8: THE FOURTEEN HELPERS",
    "header_color": "cyan",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "From here on, example sentences use a",
          "handful of little words whose own cards",
          "come later. Meet them now — recognise,",
          "don't memorise. The first seven:",
          "",
          "   sana    very, a lot",
          "   kazi    work",
          "   kitu    a thing",
          "   mji     a town",
          "   sababu  a reason",
          "   bora    better, excellent",
          "   hata    even",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Sana. Kazi. Kitu. Mji. Sababu. Bora. Hata."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "And the other seven:",
          "",
          "   tena    again",
          "   pia     also",
          "   bado    still, not yet",
          "   kidogo  a little",
          "   zaidi   more",
          "   vizuri  well",
          "   nje     outside",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "When one of these turns up in a",
          "sentence, you already know it. When",
          "its card finally arrives, it will",
          "feel like an old friend."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Tena. Pia. Bado. Kidogo. Zaidi. Vizuri. Nje."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "009",
    "trigger_word": "asubuhi",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 9: THE SWAHILI CLOCK",
    "header_color": "yellow",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "The day has four bands:",
          "",
          "   asubuhi   morning",
          "   mchana    midday, afternoon",
          "   jioni     evening",
          "   usiku     night",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "And three anchors around today:",
          "",
          "   jana - leo - kesho",
          "   yesterday - today - tomorrow",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 2"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Asubuhi. Mchana. Jioni. Usiku.",
          "2": "Jana. Leo. Kesho."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "Now the famous part. One word first:",
          "saa — hour, clock (its card comes",
          "later). On the equator the sun rises",
          "at six, every day of the year. So the",
          "Swahili day STARTS there:",
          "",
          "   saa moja asubuhi  = 7 am",
          "   (hour one of the morning)",
          "",
          "   saa sita mchana   = noon",
          "   saa kumi na mbili = 6 o'clock",
          "",
          "Hear it:                           Press 1",
          "",
          "The rule: add or subtract six from",
          "the clock on your wall."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Saa moja asubuhi. Saa sita mchana."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 3,
        "total_pages": 3,
        "content": [
          "The week counts too — starting from",
          "SATURDAY, day one:",
          "",
          "   Jumamosi   Saturday  (day 1)",
          "   Jumapili   Sunday    (day 2)",
          "   Jumatatu   Monday    (day 3!)",
          "   Jumanne    Tuesday",
          "   Jumatano   Wednesday",
          "   Alhamisi   Thursday",
          "   Ijumaa     Friday",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "Yes: Monday is 'day three'. You can",
          "see tatu and nne hiding in Jumatatu",
          "and Jumanne. The counting never stops."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Jumamosi. Jumapili. Jumatatu. Jumanne. Jumatano. Alhamisi. Ijumaa."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "010",
    "trigger_word": "soko",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 10: TO HAVE IS TO BE WITH",
    "header_color": "green",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Swahili has no verb 'to have'. You are",
          "simply WITH a thing: na = with.",
          "",
          "   nina   I have      tuna   we have",
          "   una    you have    mna    you all",
          "   ana    he/she has  wana   they have",
          "",
          "   Nina pesa.        I have money.",
          "   Una risiti?       Do you have",
          "                     a receipt?",
          "   Mteja ana deni.   The customer",
          "                     has a debt.",
          "",
          "Hear the pattern:                  Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Nina pesa. Una risiti? Mteja ana deni."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Market talk runs on two questions:",
          "",
          "   Bei gani?         What price?",
          "   Una mchele?       Got rice?",
          "",
          "And the verbs you know already:",
          "",
          "   Ninanunua mkate.  I'm buying bread.",
          "   Anauza samaki.    She sells fish.",
          "   Tunalipa sasa.    We pay now.",
          "",
          "Hear the market:                   Press 1",
          "",
          "The have-not (hana, sina) waits for",
          "the negation lessons — one machine",
          "at a time."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Bei gani? Una mchele? Ninanunua mkate. Anauza samaki."
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "lesson_id": "011",
    "trigger_word": "shule",
    "trigger_card_number": 1,
    "title": "LESSON 11: THE LITTLE -A AGREES",
    "header_color": "magenta",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "English says 'of'. Swahili says -a,",
          "and the -a wears the class of the",
          "thing that is owned:",
          "",
          "   mwalimu wa shule    (m/wa -> wa)",
          "   the school's teacher",
          "",
          "   kitabu cha somo     (ki/vi -> cha)",
          "   the book of the lesson",
          "",
          "   jibu la swali       (ji/ma -> la)",
          "   the answer to the question",
          "",
          "   kalamu ya mwanafunzi  (n -> ya)",
          "   the student's pen",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1"
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Mwalimu wa shule. Kitabu cha somo. Jibu la swali. Kalamu ya mwanafunzi."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "Plurals swap the -a form with them:",
          "",
          "   vitabu vya darasa    (vi -> vya)",
          "   majibu ya maswali    (ma -> ya)",
          "   kalamu za wanafunzi  (n pl -> za)",
          "",
          "Hear them:                         Press 1",
          "",
          "Bonus: ordinals ride this same -a.",
          "Second = pili (from mbili):",
          "",
          "   swali la pili     question two",
          "   mtihani wa pili   the second exam",
          "",
          "One little -a, the whole class system",
          "shining through it."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Vitabu vya darasa. Majibu ya maswali. Swali la pili."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
